\begin{proof}
Suppose first that $U_1\cup U_2$ is a subspace of $V$. We prove that one of $U_1,U_2$ is contained in the other. If neither containment holds, choose $v\in U_1\setminus U_2$ and $u\in U_2\setminus U_1$. Since $U_1\cup U_2$ is a subspace, $v+u\in U_1\cup U_2$.

If $v+u\in U_1$, then, because $-v\in U_1$, we have $(v+u)+(-v)=u\in U_1$, contradicting $u\notin U_1$. If $v+u\in U_2$, then, because $-u\in U_2$, we have $(v+u)+(-u)=v\in U_2$, contradicting $v\notin U_2$. Thus one of $U_1\subseteq U_2$ or $U_2\subseteq U_1$ must hold.

Conversely, suppose $U_1\subseteq U_2$ or $U_2\subseteq U_1$. If $U_1\subseteq U_2$, then $U_1\cup U_2=U_2$, which is a subspace of $V$. The other case is analogous. Therefore, $U_1\cup U_2$ is a subspace of $V$ if and only if one of the subspaces is contained in the other.
\end{proof}

\begin{proof}
We prove both implications.

First, suppose that $U_1\cup U_2$ is a subspace of $V$. Assume toward a contradiction that neither subspace contains the other. Then there exist vectors $v\in U_1\setminus U_2$ and $u\in U_2\setminus U_1$. Since $v,u\in U_1\cup U_2$ and this union is a subspace, $v+u\in U_1\cup U_2$.

If $v+u\in U_1$, then $-v\in U_1$, so $u=(v+u)+(-v)\in U_1$, contradicting the choice of $u$. If $v+u\in U_2$, then $-u\in U_2$, so $v=(v+u)+(-u)\in U_2$, contradicting the choice of $v$. Thus neither noncontainment is possible, and therefore $U_1\subseteq U_2$ or $U_2\subseteq U_1$.

Conversely, if $U_1\subseteq U_2$, then $U_1\cup U_2=U_2$, which is a subspace. If $U_2\subseteq U_1$, then $U_1\cup U_2=U_1$, which is a subspace. Consequently, the union is a subspace if and only if one of the two subspaces is contained in the other.
\end{proof}
